WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.390
In 2016 a company launched a

2
00:00:03.390 --> 00:00:05.170
chatbot that learned from

3
00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:06.590
conversations with the public.

4
00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:09.870
Within a day, coordinated users

5
00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:11.970
had taught it offensive replies,

6
00:00:12.550 --> 00:00:13.650
and it was taken offline.

7
00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:16.160
Nobody hacked the code.

8
00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:18.350
They fed it data.

9
00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:22.700
Today: how attackers change a model by

10
00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:25.240
changing its training data, the difference

11
00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:28.100
between availability, targeted and

12
00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:31.200
backdoor poisoning, and how to teach data

13
00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:33.420
provenance as a security control.

14
00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:37.770
Data poisoning means inserting or changing

15
00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:40.310
training examples so that the trained

16
00:00:40.310 --> 00:00:42.390
model behaves as the attacker wants.

17
00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:45.110
Any model trained on scraped,

18
00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:48.150
crowdsourced or user data is exposed.

19
00:00:49.630 --> 00:00:51.390
Availability attacks lower

20
00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:52.830
accuracy for everyone.

21
00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:55.560
Targeted attacks make the model

22
00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:57.160
fail on chosen inputs.

23
00:00:57.950 --> 00:01:00.590
Backdoor attacks plant a hidden trigger,

24
00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:03.850
such as a small patch, that switches the

25
00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:05.550
output only when it appears.

26
00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:09.410
Add a small yellow square to one

27
00:01:09.410 --> 00:01:11.550
percent of stop sign photos and

28
00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:13.030
label them as speed limits.

29
00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:15.760
The model stays accurate on clean

30
00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:17.700
tests, so nobody notices.

31
00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:20.920
With the square, it reads a speed limit.

32
00:01:22.190 --> 00:01:24.290
Defences start before training.

33
00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:27.380
Track where every data source comes from,

34
00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:30.760
limit who can add data, filter outliers,

35
00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:33.340
and keep a trusted held-out set.

36
00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:36.030
Clean accuracy alone cannot

37
00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:37.310
reveal a backdoor.

38
00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:40.990
Students treat datasets as

39
00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:42.650
fixed and trustworthy.

40
00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:45.910
Ask them to draw the full data supply

41
00:01:45.910 --> 00:01:48.750
chain of a model, from collection to

42
00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:52.110
labelling to download, and mark where an

43
00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:54.030
attacker could insert examples.

44
00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:55.820
Quick check!

45
00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:58.760
A model is accurate on clean

46
00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:01.560
tests but misreads any image

47
00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:03.320
with a small yellow square.

48
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:05.940
Which kind of poisoning is this?

49
00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:07.870
A backdoor.

50
00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:10.330
The trigger switches the output,

51
00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:13.210
and clean accuracy hides it.

52
00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:16.780
In the app, open the poison lab.

53
00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:19.040
Add poisoned points to the

54
00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:21.820
training set, retrain, and watch

55
00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:23.660
the decision boundary move.

56
00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:25.720
Your turn.

57
00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:28.040
A team trains on images

58
00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:29.300
scraped from the web.

59
00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:31.750
Which step best protects it

60
00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:32.410
against poisoning?

61
00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:35.300
Provenance tracking with

62
00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:37.580
filtering and a trusted test set.

63
00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:40.890
Remember: poisoning attacks the data,

64
00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:44.850
backdoors hide behind clean accuracy, and

65
00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:47.290
defence starts in the data supply chain.

66
00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:50.550
Try it in your next lecture: give

67
00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:52.890
groups the description of a spam filter

68
00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:55.250
retrained weekly on user reports.

69
00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:58.320
Each group marks every point where an

70
00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:00.460
attacker could insert data, then

71
00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:02.420
proposes one control for each.
